WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.497
Welcome to Module 6 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:07.150
onboard servers with Ansible beside Arc.

3
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:11.062
This module serves Gates 5 and
8 of the implementation guide,

4
00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:15.287
and takes Chapter 08 of Building
the Attribute-Driven Migration.

5
00:00:15.287 --> 00:00:19.371
You'll onboard one wave of servers
to Azure Arc with Microsoft's role,

6
00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:22.507
from a CMDB export, with
every server tagged with the

7
00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:25.862
tool that owns its settings
and the tool that patches it.

8
00:00:25.862 --> 00:00:29.475
And you'll write a plan a reviewer
reads before anything runs.

9
00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:35.577
The lab reads the CMDB into groups and
tags, runs Microsoft's role in check mode,

10
00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:40.725
plans a wave, dry-runs it, and breaks
the CMDB on purpose, all offline.

11
00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:46.450
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not against a production estate.

12
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.350
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:55.951
In this workbook's implementation guide,
Gate 5's server convention says an

14
00:00:55.951 --> 00:01:00.775
Arc server's tags are its attributes,
drawn from the asset record at onboarding.

15
00:01:00.775 --> 00:01:05.975
Gate 8 writes each wave as a query:
servers by resource group and tag.

16
00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:07.622
Microsoft's azure arc role,

17
00:01:07.622 --> 00:01:12.563
which Microsoft Learn documents in Connect
machines to Azure Arc at scale with

18
00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:16.756
Ansible, connects whatever hosts
the inventory names, with no waves,

19
00:01:16.756 --> 00:01:20.200
and with no dry run on a server
that doesn't have the agent.

20
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.225
And the tags are written once,
at the connect, from the CMDB.

21
00:01:24.225 --> 00:01:27.458
In this workbook's reading,
that makes the onboarding

22
00:01:27.458 --> 00:01:30.837
run the place where owners
are read, checked and carried.

23
00:01:30.837 --> 00:01:35.351
A server connected without them is open
to whichever tool reaches it first.

24
00:01:35.351 --> 00:01:39.498
In this module you read the CMDB
into groups and tags, plan a wave,

25
00:01:39.498 --> 00:01:44.090
and see what each server would
get before anything is contacted.

26
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.655
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

27
00:01:50.655 --> 00:01:53.278
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

28
00:01:53.278 --> 00:01:56.810
You'll explain why each server
records one configuration

29
00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.902
owner and one patch owner,
and where each tag is read.

30
00:01:59.902 --> 00:02:03.341
You'll describe what Microsoft's
azure arc role leaves to you.

31
00:02:03.341 --> 00:02:07.003
You'll run the CMDB inventory,
and say what makes it stop.

32
00:02:07.003 --> 00:02:10.790
You'll plan a wave and read
the plan, before anything runs.

33
00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:14.684
And you'll explain why the role can't
dry-run a server without the agent,

34
00:02:14.684 --> 00:02:16.665
and what the playbook does instead.

35
00:02:16.665 --> 00:02:18.864
Before you start, finish Module 5:

36
00:02:18.864 --> 00:02:24.077
its enforcing definitions filter on the
Config Owner tag this module writes.

37
00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:28.608
Lab 1 needs a disposable
Linux control node, or WSL,

38
00:02:28.608 --> 00:02:35.128
with Python 3.10 or later, ansible
core, ansible lint, yamllint and pytest.

39
00:02:35.128 --> 00:02:40.170
Step three runs Microsoft's role as
root against the control node itself.

40
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.470
This workbook's guidance gives
every setting one owning tool.

41
00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.698
The CMDB records it for
each asset as config owner,

42
00:02:50.698 --> 00:02:55.108
and the workbook's Arc track guide
mirrors it to the Config Owner tag.

43
00:02:55.108 --> 00:03:00.196
Patching has one owner too: a patch owner
column, mirrored to the Patch Owner tag.

44
00:03:00.196 --> 00:03:02.133
Arc doesn't change who owns what.

45
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.266
It makes the server an Azure resource,

46
00:03:04.266 --> 00:03:07.570
and the tags tell the Azure
tools which servers are theirs.

47
00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:12.434
The kit's enforcing machine configuration
definitions filter on Config Owner,

48
00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:16.045
so machine configuration changes
only the servers it owns.

49
00:03:16.045 --> 00:03:20.667
Microsoft Learn's page, About dynamic
scoping, for Azure Update Manager,

50
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:26.073
says dynamic scopes group machines by tags
and are evaluated at each scheduled run,

51
00:03:26.073 --> 00:03:28.658
so a scope on Patch Owner follows the tag.

52
00:03:28.658 --> 00:03:35.058
Take aws oh one, a Red Hat server on
AWS that Ansible configures and patches.

53
00:03:35.058 --> 00:03:38.661
It still goes to Arc, with
Patch Owner set to Ansible, so,

54
00:03:38.661 --> 00:03:43.983
in this workbook's guidance, Update
Manager assesses it and schedules nothing.

55
00:03:43.983 --> 00:03:47.968
And afterward, the kit's check V
K ARC oh seven reports any server

56
00:03:47.968 --> 00:03:53.070
machine configuration doesn't own that
carries its enforcing assignment.

