83
00:05:42.705 --> 00:05:47.842
then twenty-five percent, and keeps
production out of waves zero and one.

84
00:05:47.842 --> 00:05:51.020
It can't run in check mode on
a server without the agent;

85
00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.342
the kit's playbook doesn't call
it without a confirm flag.

86
00:05:54.342 --> 00:05:58.760
The role's code sends a signal with
the subscription and tenant by default,

87
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:02.092
which Learn's page doesn't
mention; the kit turns that off.

88
00:06:02.092 --> 00:06:04.801
And it leaves no record,
and the requirements

89
00:06:04.801 --> 00:06:07.730
file on Learn's page
installs the latest version.

90
00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.206
The verdict, which is this workbook's:

91
00:06:10.206 --> 00:06:14.530
use Microsoft's role, unchanged,
and add what sits around it.

92
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:23.592
The kit's CMDB inventory script turns
each owner into a group and a tag.

93
00:06:23.592 --> 00:06:28.694
A config owner of arc machine configuration,
Ansible, Intune and so on becomes a

94
00:06:28.694 --> 00:06:33.717
group, and a Config Owner tag in the
spelling of this workbook's Arc track guide.

95
00:06:33.717 --> 00:06:35.680
The patch owner works the same way.

96
00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:39.987
An empty owner on a tier one or
two record goes to a missing group,

97
00:06:39.987 --> 00:06:43.879
which is a finding under NIST
special publication eight

98
00:06:43.879 --> 00:06:47.855
hundred fifty-three control
CM-8, inventory, never a target.

99
00:06:47.855 --> 00:06:49.805
And the wave becomes a wave group.

100
00:06:49.805 --> 00:06:51.605
Three rules fail closed.

101
00:06:51.605 --> 00:06:54.393
An owner value the script
doesn't know stops it.

102
00:06:54.393 --> 00:06:58.502
A typo like update manager written
as one word would otherwise create a

103
00:06:58.502 --> 00:07:02.680
group nothing reads, and drop the server
out of the group that protects it.

104
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.730
A wave that isn't a number stops it.

105
00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:10.008
And a server is a candidate only if its
record has both owners, a resource group,

106
00:07:10.008 --> 00:07:12.812
a region, a wave, and
tag values Arc accepts;

107
00:07:12.812 --> 00:07:18.255
Learn's azcmagent connect reference takes
the tags as one comma-delimited list.

108
00:07:18.255 --> 00:07:21.180
Otherwise it's blocked,
with the reasons recorded.

109
00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.450
All three are the kit's design.

110
00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.800
The kit's wave plan playbook
runs only on the control node.

111
00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.163
You give it a wave number.

112
00:07:32.163 --> 00:07:37.074
It lists the wave's ready servers
and the ones the CMDB blocked,

113
00:07:37.074 --> 00:07:42.851
and writes a JSON and a CSV plan under
reports: host, configuration item,

114
00:07:42.851 --> 00:07:46.222
environment, resource
group, region, cloud,

115
00:07:46.222 --> 00:07:49.688
both owners, the exact
tag string and a state.

116
00:07:49.688 --> 00:07:53.397
It stops when no wave is given
or the wave has no ready server,

117
00:07:53.397 --> 00:07:57.182
when the cloud is wrong or a
region belongs to the other cloud,

118
00:07:57.182 --> 00:08:01.270
when a production server is planned
in wave zero or one, which this

119
00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:05.963
workbook's Arc track keeps for non-production,
and when a server has no owner.

120
00:08:05.963 --> 00:08:09.559
Both onboarding playbooks
import it as their first play,

121
00:08:09.559 --> 00:08:12.237
